## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform full-width bit reversal in constant time using SWAR patterns, not per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit composition as hi/lo 64-bit
- SWAR bit-reversal: 1,2,4,8,16,32-bit block swaps
- Final cross-half swap to complete reversal

## Code Example
```javascript
// 64-bit reversal helper
static uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}
uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to larger widths (256-bit) with minimal extra code?
- What intrinsics or compiler options help optimize this on typical CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests applying known bit-hacks to multi-lane data without branching, simulating performance-critical kernels.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting across lanes
- 128-bit to four 32-bit lanes mapping
- Branchless arithmetic
- Endianness and lane indexing

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  return x & 0x3F;
}

uint32_t popcountPerLane128(uint128 x) {
  uint32_t lo0 = (uint32_t)(x.lo);
  uint32_t lo1 = (uint32_t)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32_t hi0 = (uint32_t)(x.hi);
  uint32_t hi1 = (uint32_t)(x.hi >> 32);

  uint32_t p0 = popcount32(lo0);
  uint32_t p1 = popcount32(lo1);
  uint32_t p2 = popcount32(hi0);
  uint32_t p3 = popcount32(hi1);

  return (p3 << 24) | (p2 << 16) | (p1 << 8) | p0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 64-bit lanes?
- What are the performance implications on modern CPUs with SIMD intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates capability to manipulate multi-word integers with pure bit tricks, a common requirement in crypto and low-level systems programming. It tests cross-lane thinking and branch-free implementation.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit propagation between hi and lo.
- Branch-free 128-bit rotation using only shifts and OR.

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft1281(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  return r;
}
```

